<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Irminsul</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Irminsul was a sacred pillar, perhaps a great tree trunk, venerated by the pagan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxons</a> of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. Frankish annals record that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a> destroyed it in 772, at the start of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxon-wars">Saxon Wars</a>, after capturing the fortress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eresburg">Eresburg</a>. Its exact location and meaning are uncertain, but the 9th-century monk Rudolf of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fulda">Fulda</a> described it as a universal column supporting all things.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/irminsul.jpg" alt="Irminsul" width="320" height="494" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Heinrich Leutemann (1824-1904). · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zerst%C3%B6rung_der_Irminsaule_durch_Karl_den_Gro%C3%9Fen_by_Heinrich_Leutemann.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Old Saxon compound Irminsûl means great pillar. Its first element, Irmin, meaning great, is cognate with terms elsewhere in Germanic mythology: in Old Norse the form Jörmunr is one of the names of Odin. Sacred trees and groves were widely venerated by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germanic-peoples">Germanic peoples</a>, and the oldest chronicle describing an Irminsul calls it a tree trunk erected in the open air.</p><p>Older scholarship inferred from the name a god Irmin, presumed to have been the national god or demi-god of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxons</a>. Irmin could also have been an aspect or epithet of another deity, most likely <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/woden">Wodan</a>, or perhaps of Ziu. The view that Odin replaced Tyr as chief god, favoured by early 20th-century Nordicist writers, is not generally considered likely today.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>According to the Royal Frankish Annals, in 772 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a> repeatedly ordered the destruction of the chief seat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> religion, an Irminsul, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxon-wars">Saxon Wars</a>. It is described as not far from Heresburg, now Obermarsberg. Jacob Grimm argued that strong reasons place it about 24 km away in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutoburg-forest">Teutoburg Forest</a>.</p><p>The Benedictine monk Rudolf of Fulda, writing in 865, described a great wooden pillar erected and worshipped beneath the open sky, whose name signified a universal, all-sustaining pillar. Clive Tolley has argued that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/widukind-of-corvey">Widukind of Corvey</a>, in his Deeds of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxons</a> of about 970, describes an Irminsul put up to celebrate a Saxon victory over the Thuringians in 531: an altar to a god of victory depicted by wooden columns, whose name <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/widukind">Widukind</a> gives as Hirmin.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-pious">Louis the Pious</a> in the 9th century a stone column was dug up at Obermarsberg and moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hildesheim">Hildesheim</a> cathedral, where it was reportedly used as a candelabrum until at least the late 19th century. In the 13th century Hildesheim still commemorated the destruction on the Saturday after Laetare Sunday, when youths on the cathedral square tried to knock over a wooden object on top of a pole with sticks and stones. The custom is described elsewhere in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> too, notably at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/halberstadt">Halberstadt</a>.</p><p>The twelfth-century Kaiserchronik mentions an Irminsul three times, showing that the concept persisted in Christian times. Scholars have compared it with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tacitus">Tacitus</a>'s Pillars of Hercules and the Jupiter Columns of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>, though Rudolf Simek notes the latter were Gallo-Roman monuments found outside the area of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eresburg">Eresburg</a>. In 1929 Wilhelm Teudt proposed that a bent tree on the Externsteine relief was an Irminsul, an idea that became a symbol for anti-Christian völkisch movements.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Irminsul” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Religion was pagan: a Saxon baptismal vow of the late 8th or 9th century makes converts renounce Thunaer, Woden and Saxnot, and sacred sites included springs, groves and the <b>Irminsul</b>, a great pillar or tree near the Eresburg, destroyed by Charlemagne in 772.</blockquote><a href="/empire/saxons">Saxons · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
